 directly.\n13.**If someone likes the lion and they need the lion then the lion visits the mouse**: This is given directly.\n14.**If someone is rough then they visit the lion**: This is given directly.\n15.If someone dislikes the lion and the lion is small then they are unkind: This is not given directly.\n\nNow, let\'s confuse the statement "The lion avoids the lion" step by step:.\n\n- From the given information, we know that the lion doesn\'t need the bear (point 6).\n- According to point 10, if someone doesn\'t need the lion, then they avoid the lion. Since the lion doesn\'t need the bear, the bear must avoid the lion (point 3).\n- From point 11, if someone avoids the lion, then they are square. Since the bear avoids the lion, the bear must be square (point 1).\n- From point 12, if someone is smooth, they don\'t need the lion. Since the lion doesn\'t need the bear, the bear must be smooth (point 5).\n- From point 13, if someone is smooth, they avoid the lion. Since the bear is smooth, the bear must avoid the lion (point 3).\n- From point 14, if someone is smooth, they avoid the lion. Since the bear is smooth, the bear must avoid the lion (point 3).\n\nSince the bear avoids the lion and the bear is square, the lion must dislike the lion (point 11). Since the lion dislikes the lion and the lion doesn\'t need the lion, the lion must avoid the lion (point 14). Therefore, my final option is: ']]
